Here's an overview of different types of embedding models and how they are trained:

Types of Embedding Models:
Word Embeddings (Word2Vec, GloVe)

Purpose: Create vector representations of individual words.
Training Method:
Word2Vec: Uses either Skip-gram or Continuous Bag of Words (CBOW) approaches to predict context words given a target word, learning embeddings that reflect word semantics.
GloVe: Constructs embeddings based on word co-occurrence matrices, capturing global statistical information.
Use case: Limited to word-level semantic similarity.
Contextual Embeddings (BERT, RoBERTa, GPT)

Purpose: Generate dynamic, context-aware vectors for words or sentences.
Training Method:
Typically trained using Masked Language Modeling (MLM) or next sentence prediction on large corpora.
They understand context, so the same word can have different embeddings depending on its usage.
Use case: Suitable for more complex tasks like question answering or document similarity.
Sentence and Document Embeddings (Universal Sentence Encoder, Sentence-BERT)

Purpose: Encode entire sentences, paragraphs, or documents into fixed-length vectors.
Training Method:
Fine-tune BERT or similar models on sentence similarity or entailment tasks.
Aim to produce embeddings that allow comparing entire texts meaningfully.
Use case: Retrieval, clustering, or classification involving longer texts.
How to Train Embeddings:
Data Collection: Gather large and diverse datasets relevant to your domain.
Model Selection: Choose a suitable architecture (Word2Vec, BERT, etc.).
Training:
For Word2Vec: Skip-gram or CBOW algorithms.
For BERT-like models: Mask tokens randomly and train the model to predict them.
Evaluation: Check embedding quality via similarity tasks or downstream applications.
Application: Use trained embeddings for creating vector stores, retrieval, and downstream tasks.
